{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"**Do not endorse `beb9cc97` for landing yet. Two P1 residuals remain, plus a validator gap.** Reviewed `3cd78223..beb9cc97` statically; no tests run or files modified.\n\n1. **P1-a: nested pass-through loses the operand’s actual origin.**  \n   `expand-call` captures the replaced application’s origin once at [macro.cljc:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1087), then assigns that origin to **every retained input address** at [macro.cljc:1137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1137). An input packet can already contain mixed origins.\n\n   Static counterexample:\n\n   ```clojure\n   (defmacro pass [x] x)\n   (defmacro wrap [x]\n     (yin/application (yin/variable 'pass) [x]))\n   (wrap (+ 1 2))\n   ```\n\n   `wrap` generates `(pass …)` with origin `Ewrap`, retaining the admitted `(+ 1 2)` operand with source origin `S`. When `pass` expands, `admitted-origin` becomes `Ewrap`; returning `x` incorrectly relabels the admitted `+` application as `Ewrap`.\n\n   Retained occurrences must preserve **their own incoming provenance**, rather than inherit the enclosing call’s origin. The final path walk therefore does not yet establish correct §2.3 correspondence.\n\n2. **P1-b: the default token is not never-repeating.**  \n   [publish.cljc:119](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:119) initializes a namespace-global counter to zero; [publish.cljc:143](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:143) uses the same fixed medium everywhere. Two fresh processes publishing identical specifications at the same counter position produce identical admission origins—and therefore can produce identical tables, records, and manifests. Restarting the publisher has the same problem.\n\n   The new test exercises two calls within one process only ([clojure_callsite_test.cljc:732](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc:732)). Require composition-supplied durable admission identities, or an explicitly scoped allocator whose identities survive independent publishers/restarts. The private global atom also conflicts with the repository’s [no hidden global/shared mutable state rules](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/docs/design/datom.world.md:26).\n\n3. **P2: mixed-origin validation accepts two markers for one occurrence.**  \n   Conflict detection groups by `[origin root path]` at [clojure.cljc:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:193), but mixed-origin coverage discards origins into a set at [clojure.cljc:220](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:220). Consequently, adding another version-1 marker with a different valid origin for an already-covered `[root path]` passes—even with `:declared? true`. Reject multiple origin claims for the same occurrence within the declared table.\n\nThe other parts of the amendments are sound:\n\n- **The corrected `unless` finding is correct.** `yin/if` constructs an `:if` and directly incorporates its operand packets ([macro.cljc:687](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:687)); the admitted `(+ 1 2)` is passed through. A changed enclosing root does not make that application generated. Retaining its source origin is consistent with [§2.1:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:38). I withdraw the earlier fixture-specific objection.\n- **Final-path tracking is appropriate for the final tail/stand-in passes.** Tail marking changes flags while preserving child positions; stand-in lowering replaces leaves with literals ([macro.cljc:877](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:877), [macro.cljc:919](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:919)). The problem is the provenance supplied to that tracking.\n- **Explicit admission tokens and recorded-origin retrieval are the right design.** Given distinct supplied tokens, admissions remain distinct. Retrieval reads `:yang.call-site/origin` from the record, checks its shape and the record’s root/table/version, and does not reconstruct identity from content ([publish.cljc:407](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:407)). Repeated retrieval correctly replays that recorded identity.\n\n**Consumer check:** No direct oracle read-path regression found: it still constructs its own stamped site table and reads that table for attribution ([latte_oracle_test.clj:2464](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2464), [:2394](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2394)). Publication closure traversal still follows both table and record references ([closure.cljc:203](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc:203)); its comment at line 201 about reconstructing origins is now stale. The blocking publication defect is identity allocation, not retrieval."}}
